  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "The rate at which the population of yeast cells in a laboratory culture grows $Y(t),$ after $t$ days, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}Y}{\\text{d}t}=0.55Y.\n$$\n\nHow many days does it take for the population to triple? Round your answer to the nearest day.",
  "worked_solution": "We model the population growth using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}Y}{\\text{d}t}=0.55Y.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nY(t)={Y}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.55t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Y}_{0}$ is the initial population.\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the population is triple the initial number. That is,\n\n$$\nY({t}_{1})=3{Y}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y({t}_{1}) & ={Y}_{0}{e}^{0.55{t}_{1}} \\\\ 3{Y}_{0} & ={Y}_{0}{e}^{0.55{t}_{1}} \\\\ 3 & ={e}^{0.55{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u20613 & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{0.55{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u20613 & =0.55{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20613}{0.55} \\\\ {t}_{1} & \u22481.997\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $2$ days for the population to triple.",
